## Why This Question Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of crash-consistency semantics, cross-socket memory ordering on x86-64 systems, and practical persistent memory programming patterns.

## Key Concepts
- **Persistent Memory Semantics**: Understanding cache line flushes (clwb/clflushopt) and memory ordering fences (sfence/mfence)
- **Crash-Consistency Guarantees**: Ensuring the queue survives power loss without data corruption
- **Sequence Counter Design**: Handling wrap-around and enforcing total ordering across concurrent producers
- **Recovery Mechanisms**: Log-based recovery and ring buffer reconstruction after failure
- **Correctness Under Contention**: Two producers racing to reserve slots requires careful synchronization

## Code Example
```c
// Enqueue: reserve tail slot atomically
uint64_t tail = atomic_fetch_add(&q->tail, 1);
Slot* slot = &q->slots[tail % N];

// Spin until slot is available
while (atomic_load(&slot->seq) != tail) { cpu_pause(); }

// Write payload to PMEM
memcpy(slot->payload, data, len);

// Persist in order: payload then sequence
atomic_store(&slot->seq, tail + 1);
clflushopt(slot);      // flush cache line to PMEM
sfence();               // ensure durability
```

## Why This Is Asked
Evaluates understanding of durability guarantees, lock-free algorithms, and recovery mechanisms in persistent memory systems.

## Key Concepts
- Crash consistency and copy-on-write techniques
- Lock-free updates using compare-and-swap operations
- Epoch-based memory reclamation for concurrent access
- Persistence ordering with CLWB/SFENCE and durable pointers
- Recovery via redo log replay and pointer validation
- NVRAM-specific challenges (wear leveling, power loss) and performance considerations

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct Node {
  int key;
  struct Node **forward; // Next pointers per level
  uint64_t version;       // Node version for validation
  bool marked;           // Tombstone flag for deletion
} Node;

// Lock-free insert with crash consistency
bool nvml_insert(Node **head, int key, int level) {
  Node *new_node = allocate_nv_node(key, level);
  // Populate and persist node
  clwb(new_node, sizeof(Node));
  sfence();

  for (int l = 0; l < level; l++) {
    Node *pred, *curr;
    do {
      pred = head[l];
      curr = pred->forward[l];
    } while (!validate_link(pred, curr));

    new_node->forward[l] = curr;
    if (!cas(&pred->forward[l], curr, new_node)) {
      continue; // Retry on conflict
    }
    clwb(&pred->forward[l], sizeof(Node*));
  }
  sfence();
  return true;
}
```

